# ── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A Postgres connection string. This app talks to Postgres *directly* (Drizzle
# over node-postgres), because it relies on transactions, generated columns and
# partial unique indexes that a REST layer cannot express.
#
# ⚠ SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY are NOT this. They are the REST
#   API endpoint and a JWT for it — different credentials entirely, and neither
#   can open a Postgres connection.
#
# Supabase → Project Settings → Database → Connection string → URI
#
#   Session pooler (recommended — works over IPv4, supports migrations):
#     postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
#   Direct (IPv6 only on new projects; often unreachable from home networks):
#     postgresql://postgres:<password>@db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432/postgres
#
# The password is URL-encoded — a literal `@`, `#` or `/` in it must be written
# %40, %23 or %2F, or the URL parser will read it as the host separator.
#
# No `search_path` is needed. Every table lives in the `app` schema and is
# addressed by name (see src/db/schema/_schema.js), so this works unchanged
# against the transaction pooler on :6543, which ignores connection options.
#
# Verify it with:  npm run db:check
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# ── Clerk ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Clerk owns authentication: credentials, sessions, MFA, the sign-in UI.
# `clerk init` writes these two for you; find them again at
# dashboard.clerk.com → API keys.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=""
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=""

# Signing secret for the Clerk → database sync webhook.
# dashboard.clerk.com → Webhooks → add an endpoint pointing at
#   https://<your-domain>/api/webhooks/clerk
# subscribed to user.created, user.updated, user.deleted — then copy the secret.
#
# Optional in development: accounts are also provisioned lazily on first
# request. The webhook keeps profile edits and deletions in sync.
CLERK_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET=""

NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_URL="/sign-in"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_URL="/sign-up"

# ── Platform ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated allowlist. These emails get the ADMIN role on first sign-in.
# After bootstrap, manage admins in the `admin_allowlist` table instead.
ADMIN_EMAILS="you@example.com"

# All business dates (delivery days, billing months) resolve in this zone.
APP_TIMEZONE="Asia/Kolkata"

# Shared secret for the cron endpoints: Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>
CRON_SECRET=""

# ── Supabase REST (optional, unused today) ────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed if you later add Supabase Storage or Realtime. The application
# itself never uses them — it goes straight to Postgres via DATABASE_URL.
# SUPABASE_URL=""
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""
